        "This sequence illustrates both the upside and downside of buying and reselling expensive assets.\nWhat the car experiments teach\n- A good purchase price creates room for resale.\n- A vehicle can have hidden mechanical and ownership costs.\n- A low auction price does not guarantee a good deal.\n- A desirable item is not necessarily a profitable item.\n- Resale value depends on condition, documentation, market demand, and timing.\n- A single bad purchase can erase the gains from several smaller wins.\nCalculate the full cost of ownership\nFor any vehicle purchase, calculate:\nTotal cost = Purchase price + Taxes and registration + Repairs + Insurance + Transport + Other ownership costs\nThen estimate realistic resale proceeds after negotiation and selling expenses.",
        "A vehicle that sells for more than its purchase price may still have produced a loss after repairs, insurance, taxes, financing, and depreciation.\nUse a vehicle inspection process\nBefore buying:\n1. Verify ownership and title documents.\n2. Check for liens, restrictions, or unresolved claims.\n3. Review the service and accident history where available.\n4. Arrange an independent mechanical inspection.\n5. Check for warning lights, leaks, unusual noises, and drivetrain problems.\n6. Estimate repairs using written quotes.\n7. Compare similar completed sales, not only asking prices.\n8. Set a maximum purchase price.\n9. Walk away if the seller or documentation creates doubt.\nAuction purchases require extra caution because inspection access and return rights may be limited.\nA capital-allocation rule\nDo not risk a large portion of your available savings on an asset you cannot properly inspect or afford to repair.",
