        "The next attempt moved toward a fashion and accessories store, promoted through Instagram pages focused on fashion.",
        "The important lesson is that early stores often reveal what not to do:\n- Do not combine unrelated products without a clear customer reason.\n- Do not choose a product only because other stores are selling it.\n- Do not assume a niche audience automatically guarantees demand.\n- Do not spend heavily before understanding acquisition costs and margins.\n- Do not treat a store launch as proof of product-market fit.\nChoose a coherent niche\nA niche gives the store a reason to exist for a particular customer group.",
        "A useful niche has:\n- A recognisable audience.\n- A clear interest, need, or identity.\n- Products that naturally belong together.\n- A way to reach the audience.\n- Enough demand to support sales.\n- A credible reason to buy from your store rather than another seller.\nA niche does not have to be extremely narrow, but the products and customer should make sense together.\nProduct selection checklist\nBefore adding a product, investigate:\n1. Who is likely to buy it?\n2. Why would they want it?\n3. Is demand visible beyond one viral post?\n4. How competitive is the market?\n5. Can you source it reliably?\n6. What is the landed cost?\n7. How long will delivery take?\n8. What are the return and defect risks?\n9. Can you make a useful product page and creative?\n10. What is the realistic contribution after marketing and fulfilment?\nUnderstand the unit economics\nFor an e-commerce order:\nContribution per order = Net sales revenue - Product cost - Shipping - Payment fees - Fulfilment - Expected returns and refunds - Variable advertising cost\nDo not scale based on revenue alone. A store can generate impressive sales while losing money on every order."
