        "This was a turning point because it showed that a store could generate sales through distribution beyond the operator's own audience.\nWhy the larger placement may have worked\nA larger page can provide:\n- More exposure.\n- A concentrated audience.\n- A stronger opportunity to generate traffic quickly.\nBut follower count alone does not make a placement valuable. The audience must be relevant, real, and likely to respond to the offer.",
        "A smaller page with a highly engaged audience may outperform a larger page with weak audience fit.\nEvaluate a shoutout before buying\nAsk the page owner for:\n- Recent reach and impression data.\n- Audience location and language.\n- Audience age range, where relevant.\n- Story or post performance.\n- Examples of previous sponsored placements.\n- Pricing and exact deliverables.\n- Publication date and duration.\n- Whether the post will remain live.\n- Whether the page uses artificial engagement.\nDo not rely only on screenshots selected by the seller. Where possible, verify data through platform analytics or a live screen-share.\nCalculate break-even sales\nSuppose a promotion costs $300. If the store contributes $15 per order after product and fulfilment costs but before the promotion, then:\nBreak-even orders = 300 ÷ 15 = 20\nThe promotion needs 20 orders to recover its $300 cost under these assumptions.",
